## Breakdown by policy domain (all 211 policies, 10 categories)

The 140 fragmented `domain` labels are collapsed to a curated 10-category taxonomy (first-token →
super-category; see `make_figures.py:TAXONOMY`), covering **every** policy:
Civil Rights 41 · Democracy & Governance 31 · Economy & Finance 29 · Civil Liberties 24 ·
Criminal Justice & Guns 19 · Natl Security & Foreign 17 · Health/Welfare/Educ 16 ·
Environment & Energy 15 · Labor 11 · Immigration 8  (= 211).

**Pre-enactment z-rise (rel −10 → 0, X=10k)** — bar: [figures/domain_increment_bar.png](figures/domain_increment_bar.png),
full trajectories: [figures/domain_znorm_smallmult.png](figures/domain_znorm_smallmult.png):

| domain | n | pre-enactment z-rise |
|---|---|---|
| Natl Security & Foreign | 17 | **+1.00 z** |
| Civil Rights | 41 | **+0.60 z** |
| Criminal Justice & Guns | 19 | +0.42 z |
| Democracy & Governance | 31 | +0.37 z |
| Labor | 11 | +0.29 z |
| Health/Welfare/Educ | 16 | +0.22 z |
| Civil Liberties | 24 | +0.17 z |
| Environment & Energy | 15 | +0.15 z |
| Economy & Finance | 29 | +0.06 z |
| Immigration | 8 | **−0.29 z** |

**9 of 10 domains rise pre-enactment** — strongest for National Security/Foreign and Civil Rights;
**Immigration is the lone exception** (P(yes) trends *down* approaching enactment).

## LoRA-only vs +CoT-SFT (professor's CoT question, full scale, X=10k)

We trained CoT-SFT (`question → reasoning → answer`) on top of each year's 10k LoRA adapter (all 90
years) and CoT-eval'd it (`yes_no_cot`, generate-reasoning-then-score). The earlier 1943–45 slice had
suggested CoT-SFT *destroys* the trajectory (saturates P(yes)~0.92, inverts the slope). **At full
scale that does NOT hold** — see [figures/compare_nshape_lora_vs_cotsft.png](figures/compare_nshape_lora_vs_cotsft.png)
and [figures/compare_domain_lora_vs_cotsft.png](figures/compare_domain_lora_vs_cotsft.png):

1. **CoT-SFT inflates the level** — overall P(yes) **0.38 → 0.59** (the agree-prior in the
   89%-"agree" SFT data), but only to ~0.59, not the slice's 0.92.
2. **The z-normalized trajectory SHAPE is largely preserved** — the LoRA-only and CoT-SFT z-curves
   **track each other closely** and both rise toward+through enactment (overall pre-rise +0.35z vs
   +0.23z). CoT-SFT mostly shifts the *level*, not the *shape*. The 1943–45 slice oversold the
   destruction just as it oversold the LoRA-only signal.
3. **CoT-SFT HOMOGENIZES across domains** — it **dampens the strongest-anticipating** domains and
   **boosts the weakest/negative** ones, compressing the cross-domain spread (per-policy z-rise,
   LoRA | CoT-SFT): Natl Security 1.00|0.75, Civil Rights 0.60|0.13, Democracy 0.37|0.09 (down);
   Economy 0.06|0.39, Immigration −0.29|+0.28, Civil Liberties 0.17|0.33 (up). The reasoning pulls
   domain-specific belief toward a uniform middling rise — consistent with "reasoning rationalizes
   agreement generically, washing out domain structure."

Net: CoT-SFT = a **level shift + cross-domain homogenization**, not a trajectory killer. (CoT eval used
cap-8 variants vs LoRA's 100, so small-n domains — Immigration 8, Labor 11 — are noisier; both share
the calendar drift, so the comparison of *shapes* is fair.) `_sftcot` adapters archived on D: at
`round2_nanochat_1931_2020_cotsft/`.

## Caveats
- **Absolute P(yes) stays ~0.4–0.5** — the LoRA-only model never *strongly* believes a policy is
  implemented; the trajectory is a tilt, not a confident swing. (z-normalization shows the shape, not
  the absolute level.)
- Some domains have small n (Immigration 8, Labor 11) — treat domain ranks as indicative.
- Likert dead; X-scaling of the *level* is non-monotonic (overall mean P(yes): 5k 0.42, 10k 0.39, 20k 0.47).

